# stair — the interior grand stair, 1.80 to 6.30, twenty-five risers in two flights round a half
#         landing, with a turned balustrade, a moulded handrail and worn treads.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — read this before you move a single number.
#
# THE HEAD-HEIGHT CHECK UNDER A FLIGHT OF STAIRS. It is the one geometric constraint in this
# building that cannot be satisfied by eye, and it is the only one the engine's own `walkability`
# CANNOT CHECK FOR YOU. measure.cpp walks a HEIGHTFIELD: for every column it finds the topmost
# non-air voxel and asks for clear air above it. A floor under an overhang is not the topmost
# matter in its column, so it is not a surface, so it is never tested. Stand under this stair and
# `walkable` has nothing to say about you. Written up in clips/facility/requests/stair.md.
#
# So this file measures it itself, and the measurement is two bindings you can run:
#
#     bash tools/clipcheck.sh clips/facility.clip --part stair_probe_head --metre 8   -> EMPTY
#     bash tools/clipcheck.sh clips/facility.clip --part stair_rig --metre 8 --gap y@7.40,5.50
#
# `stair_probe_head` is the volume a person sweeps through every part of this stair — 2.40 m of
# head height over every floor, tread and landing, and never less than 1.20 m of width between the
# things standing beside them — INTERSECTED with the stair. IT MUST REPORT AN EMPTY CLIP. Anything
# it reports is a place somebody hits their head or their shoulder. `stair_rig` is the same stair
# with the floor, the two walls and a lid put back, so that `--gap` reports the storey rather than
# the seven metres of air between the gallery and the roof; see its own note at the bottom.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THE RISER SPLIT AND THE PLAN BOTH MOVED
#
# The stair that stood here cleared 2.115 m under its half landing and was 0.945 m wide between
# its handrails, and it had a 0.68 x 1.05 m HOLE IN THE HALF LANDING that nothing in this file
# could see. Three things moved, and every one of them is forced by a measurement rather than by
# taste.
#
#   EIGHTEEN RISERS TO THE HALF LANDING AND SEVEN ABOVE IT, and it used to be thirteen and twelve.
#   Two constraints set this, and the second is the one nobody had found:
#
#     head height.  The landing carries the passage's ceiling on its back. Thirteen risers put its
#     top at 4.14, and less the thinnest slab the module allows that leaves 2.115 m under it —
#     285 mm short of the 2.40 this building is now held to.
#
#     THE ARCHED NORTH WINDOW AT x = +-8.10 WAS EATING THE LANDING. windows.clip cuts its openings
#     1.80 m in from the outer face (`windows_ga_cut`, a box to y 4.05 and a cylinder of r 0.675
#     on top of it, so the opening's head is at 4.725 and the cut reaches z 5.70). The half landing
#     is 1.80 m deep and the north openings stand on the 2.70 bay with 1.35 m of clear wall between
#     them, so NO x POSITION EXISTS where a landing this deep misses one. The only way out is
#     upwards: the landing's soffit has to clear 4.725. Sixteen risers gives 4.455 and is eaten;
#     seventeen with a 0.225 slab gives 4.635 and is eaten; EIGHTEEN gives 4.815 and is clear by
#     0.09. That is the whole of why this flight is eighteen risers long.
#
#     EIGHTEEN RISERS IN ONE FLIGHT IS MORE THAN A PUBLIC STAIR SHOULD HAVE and it is the price of
#     somebody else's cutter. clips/facility/requests/walls-thickness.md asks windows.clip to cut
#     `wall_cut` = 1.00 instead of 1.80; when it does, a sixteen-riser landing is still nibbled
#     0.10 m at its north edge, because the opening's HEAD at 4.725 is the opening's own dimension
#     and not the cut's depth. Eighteen is clear either way, which is why it is written this way
#     rather than as a workaround with a date on it.
#
#   THE FLIGHTS STAND AGAINST THE WALLS, one each, and the well between them is M and not 2 M.
#   The band this stair has is z 2.925 (halls.clip's block face) to 6.60 (the north wall), which
#   is 3.675 m and cannot grow: the halls' side wall is a whole diameter thick because it carries
#   a groin vault, and the north wall is the outside of the building. Two flights with a
#   balustrade on both sides and a 0.90 well spends 1.4175 of that on handrails and air and
#   leaves 0.945 a flight. Two flights with a wall on one side and a 0.45 well spends 0.855 and
#   leaves 1.3575 and 1.4475. So the lower flight is bedded 0.065 into the halls block and takes
#   its south side off it, the upper flight takes its north side off the north wall, and each
#   carries ONE balustrade, on the well.
#
#   THE HALF LANDING IS 1.80 DEEP AND NOT 1.35. You cross a half landing between its outer
#   balustrade and the guard over the well, and those two eat 0.405 of it between them. At 1.35
#   the crossing was 0.945 m — the same failure as the flights, in the one place on a stair where
#   somebody is turning round. At 1.80 (4 M) it is 1.395.
#
# The tightest clearance in the finished stair is 3.015 m under the half landing and 1.3575 m
# across the lower flight. Both have a whole voxel of margin at metre 8 and four at metre 32,
# which is the point of moving them: the old 15 mm was half a voxel at the contract's own metre
# and could not be measured, only asserted.
#
# Secondary loads, all of them free riders on geometry that had to exist anyway:
#
#   THE BALUSTERS. Fifty-one of the order's `ionic_baluster` to a stair, a hundred and two in the
#   building, each with a 0.063 m neck — two voxels at metre 32, the second-thinnest matter in the
#   building — and TWENTY-THREE OF THEM ARE ON A RAKE, so no two are at the same height and none
#   shares a voxel row with its neighbour. Each now carries a bronze SHOE and COLLAR as well, one
#   voxel deep and standing proud of a turned member: a metal fixing against a stone one, in the
#   same voxel column, which is a harder question for the material sampler than either alone.
#
#   THE WORN TREADS. Twenty-three dished hollows to a stair, each a flattened ellipsoid 36 mm deep
#   sunk into a face the walkability check measures its worst rise on. A dish is a curved surface
#   whose gradient turns over inside one voxel at its rim, on the one surface in this building
#   whose height is a reported number, and it must not move that number: the dish is inset 0.06
#   from the nosing at both ends so the rise from tread to tread is still exactly 0.18.
#
#   THE ROTATED FRAME, six times: each raking handrail, each raking plinth, each raking wall
#   string and the upper flight's soffit are straight sections built about the origin and turned
#   by atan(0.18/0.32) about z. Rotations are isometries so the field stays exact and keeps a box.
#
#   A ROOM ONLY LIGHT CAN GET INTO SIDEWAYS. The passage under the stair is 3.015 m high, 24 m2,
#   and its only daylight is the two ground-storey north windows at x = 10.80 and 13.50, whose
#   heads are at 4.50 — under the whole of the upper flight, so everything below the half landing
#   is lit by bounce off a floor. That floor is now LAID IN FLAGS: `void_stair` is a grid of 36 mm
#   joints on the 0.90 (D) square cut into the podium, which is the only matter this part removes
#   from the building and the only reason it needs a void at all.
# ================================================================================================
#
# WHERE IT IS, AND WHY IT IS THERE
#
# The north strip of the east wing, mirrored to the west. halls.clip has the middle of both wings
# (x 4.95..13.95, z +-2.925, up to 6.975) and says in writing that the strips z 2.925..6.60 are
# left for somebody else. This is that strip: 3.675 m deep, which is why the stair runs east-west
# and not north-south — a flight is 5.44 m long and would not fit across it.
#
# TWO STAIRS AND NOT ONE. The brief says "in one wing"; BRIEF.md rule 3 says symmetric about x = 0
# and does not qualify it for the shell. One stair in the east wing and none in the west is the one
# thing that would read as a mistake from the plan, so the whole part is `mirror { } axis=x` and
# there is a matching stair in each wing. Everything below is built on POSITIVE x, because mirror
# folds the coordinate to its absolute value and a shape drawn on -x simply disappears.
#
# WHAT IT KEEPS OUT OF, and this is now a list of measured clearances rather than an assumption.
# windows.clip cuts 1.80 m in from the outer faces at x = +-16.00 and z = +-7.50, so its openings
# reach x = +-14.20 and z = +-5.70. On the north wall they stand at x = +-2.70, +-5.40, +-8.10
# (arched, y 2.70..4.725), and at +-10.80, +-13.50 (square, y 2.70..4.50); on the end walls at
# z = 0, +-2.70, +-5.40 (y 2.70..4.50 and 6.30..8.55). Each is 1.35 wide. So:
#
#   - the half landing's soffit is at 4.815, which is 0.09 OVER the head of the arched opening it
#     stands across. That single number is why this flight has eighteen risers in it.
#   - the lower flight is entirely south of z = 4.50 and west of x = 13.54, so no north opening and
#     no end-wall opening can reach it at all.
#   - the upper flight ends at x = 10.07, west of the square opening at 10.80 whose reveal begins
#     at 10.125, and its soffit never falls below 4.815.
#   - THE TALL PIERS STAND AT x 11.61..12.06, in the 1.35 m of solid wall between the openings at
#     10.80 and 13.50, and NOT under the gallery edge they look as though they carry. A pier under
#     that edge would be inside the opening at 10.80 and would be cut off at the knees by
#     `void_windows` without a word. The gallery is carried by the upper flight along z 4.95..6.75
#     and cantilevers only the 0.45 of the well.
#   - the gallery slab is y 5.895..6.30 and every opening's band is 2.70..4.50 or 6.30..8.55, so it
#     lies in the 1.80 m of blind wall between them.
#   - THE ONE THING THAT IS CLOSE: the gallery's south handrail, at z 4.4775..4.7025, passes the
#     end-wall opening at z = 5.40 whose reveal begins at 4.725. That is 22 mm — 0.7 of a voxel at
#     metre 32. It is clear, it was clear before, and it is the only member in this file with less
#     than 0.09 to give. Do not move that rail north.
#
# WHERE IT MEETS ITS NEIGHBOURS
#
#   podium.clip      the floor at 1.80. Everything that stands is founded at 1.75, and the flag
#                    joints of `void_stair` are cut 42 mm into its top.
#   walls.clip       north inner face z = 6.60, east inner face x = 15.10 — `wall_thick` 0.90 off
#                    the contract's block outline, stated at the top of that file since 2026-08-16.
#                    The half landing, the upper flight and the gallery are bedded 0.15 into them.
#   halls.clip       its block face is z = 2.925, grown to 2.940 by its own round, and its top is
#                    6.975. The lower flight and the half landing are bedded 0.065 into it, which
#                    is what lets them take a wall on that side instead of a balustrade. NOTHING
#                    OF MINE NEEDS THAT BLOCK CUT: it is solid to 6.975 there, and the 65 mm I
#                    stand in is 65 mm of a 900 mm wall.
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45. Every number below is a multiple or simple fraction of one of
# those, or of the stair's own two contract numbers, the riser 0.18 (0.4 M) and the run 0.32.
# The ones that are neither are constructions and not dimensions, and each is named where it
# occurs: the rake 0.08154932 turns (atan(0.5625)/tau), the flight ends at 10.02 and 13.54 (the
# foot plus six and seventeen runs), and the clipping frames, which are only knives.
#
#   6.30 = 14 M   half landing west face      1.80  floor              podium_top
#   8.10 = 18 M   half landing east face      1.75  founding, 0.05 into the podium
#  10.02         upper flight head            4.815 landing soffit     5.04 - M/2
#  13.54         lower flight foot            5.04  half landing       1.80 + 18 x 0.18
#  15.10         east wall inner face         5.895 gallery soffit     6.30 - 0.9 M
#  15.25         0.15 into that wall          6.30  upper floor        1.80 + 25 x 0.18
#   2.875        south face, 0.065 into halls 0.925 handrail crown over the surface a baluster
#   2.940        the halls block's own face                            stands on: 0.70 + M/2
#   4.50 = 10 M  lower flight / well          4.725 the arched opening's head — the number the
#   4.95 = 11 M  well / upper flight                riser split is decided by
#   6.60         north wall inner face
#   6.75 = 15 M  0.15 into that wall

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE LOWER FLIGHT — eighteen risers, 1.80 to 5.04, ascending WEST, on a solid spandrel
# ================================================================================================
#
# Seventeen treads of 0.32 from x = 13.54 back to 8.10, the eighteenth riser being the half landing
# itself. Seventeen nested boxes, each set back one run and raised one riser from the one below and
# each running west to x = 8.00 so that it is buried in the landing — the same construction the
# great steps use, and for the same reason: the whole value of a stair is that its treads are
# IDENTICAL, and a box is the only thing in this language that cannot drift.
#
#   tread  1   x 13.54   top 1.98        tread 10   x 10.66   top 3.60
#   tread  2   x 13.22   top 2.16        tread 11   x 10.34   top 3.78
#   tread  3   x 12.90   top 2.34        tread 12   x 10.02   top 3.96
#   tread  4   x 12.58   top 2.52        tread 13   x  9.70   top 4.14
#   tread  5   x 12.26   top 2.70        tread 14   x  9.38   top 4.32
#   tread  6   x 11.94   top 2.88        tread 15   x  9.06   top 4.50
#   tread  7   x 11.62   top 3.06        tread 16   x  8.74   top 4.68
#   tread  8   x 11.30   top 3.24        tread 17   x  8.42   top 4.86
#   tread  9   x 10.98   top 3.42        landing    x  8.10   top 5.04
#
# RISER 0.18 AND GOING 0.32, WHICH IS BRIEF.md RULE 6 AND NOT A CHOICE. 2R + G = 0.68 m and the
# pitch is atan(0.18/0.32) = 29.36 degrees — a shallow public-building stair that a person walks
# rather than climbs, and a riser a third of the 0.55 the player can step up. The nosing below adds
# 0.045 of overhang, so the tread a foot actually lands on is 0.365 deep against a 0.30 m body
# radius.
#
# They all reach down to 1.75, which makes the flight a SOLID SPANDREL rather than a raking slab.
# That is not laziness. The wedge under a flight is between zero and 3 m of head height and there
# is no honest way to let anybody in there; filling it is what a stone stair on a spandrel actually
# is, it carries the west end of the half landing, and it takes that whole volume out of the
# head-height probe by making it not a place.
#
# EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS 0.015 INSIDE THE FACE IT MAKES, and that is not a typo. `round=r` on a
# box GROWS it: the flat faces move out by r and the arris is turned with radius r through the
# corner you wrote, so a box written to a tread top of 1.98 has its tread at 1.995. The half
# landing is written the same way, and 15 mm off its soffit used to be 15 mm off the only
# clearance in this building that had 15 mm to give. It has 615 mm now, and the idiom stays
# because the treads are what the facility's worst-rise number is measured on.
let stair_a_01 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   13.525 1.965 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_02 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   13.205 2.145 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_03 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   12.885 2.325 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_04 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   12.565 2.505 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_05 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   12.245 2.685 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_06 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   11.925 2.865 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_07 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   11.605 3.045 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_08 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   11.285 3.225 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_09 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   10.965 3.405 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_10 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   10.645 3.585 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_11 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   10.325 3.765 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_12 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890   10.005 3.945 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_13 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890    9.685 4.125 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_14 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890    9.365 4.305 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_15 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890    9.045 4.485 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_16 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890    8.725 4.665 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_17 = box 8.015 1.765 2.890    8.405 4.845 4.485 round=0.015

let stair_a_steps = union {
    stair_a_01 stair_a_02 stair_a_03 stair_a_04 stair_a_05 stair_a_06 stair_a_07 stair_a_08
    stair_a_09 stair_a_10 stair_a_11 stair_a_12 stair_a_13 stair_a_14 stair_a_15 stair_a_16
    stair_a_17
}

# --- the nosing, which is a section and not an arris --------------------------------------------
#
# A tread that stops dead on its riser is a slab. A real one overhangs the riser below it by about
# a nosing's own thickness and is coved back to the riser under the overhang, and both of those are
# matter at this metre: the overhang is 0.045 (M/10) and the bar is 0.048 tall with a 0.015 bullnose
# turned on it, so the deepest shadow on the whole flight is one and a half voxels wide and there
# are seventeen of them in a line.
#
# The bar is written 0.015 inside its own faces for the same reason the treads are, so its top face
# lands exactly on the tread it belongs to and adds nothing to the rise. The cove under it is a
# `cavetto` — build_moulding centres the hollow on the SECOND corner, so the first is written at
# the riser face where the stone is and the second at the outer bottom of the overhang where it
# is not.
let stair_nose_a_bar  = box 13.495 1.917 2.890   13.570 1.965 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_nose_a_cove = cavetto 13.540 1.902 2.875   13.585 1.845 4.500 run=z
let stair_nose_a_1    = union { stair_nose_a_bar stair_nose_a_cove }
let stair_nose_a_2    = union { stair_nose_a_1  translate { stair_nose_a_1 } -0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_nose_a_4    = union { stair_nose_a_2  translate { stair_nose_a_2 } -0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_nose_a_8    = union { stair_nose_a_4  translate { stair_nose_a_4 } -1.28 0.72 0 }
let stair_nose_a_16   = union { stair_nose_a_8  translate { stair_nose_a_8 } -2.56 1.44 0 }
let stair_nose_a = union { stair_nose_a_16  translate { stair_nose_a_1 } -5.12 2.88 0 }

# --- and the hollow four hundred years of feet have worn in the middle of each one ---------------
#
# A flattened ellipsoid sunk 36 mm into the centre of every tread, 0.20 along the going and 1.00
# across the width — which is where feet actually fall, and it is not the whole tread: a stair worn
# corner to corner is a ramp, and the worn part of a real one stops short of both the nosing and the
# wall. Inset 0.06 from the nosing at both ends ON PURPOSE. The heightfield the walkability check
# builds reads the top of each column, and if the dish reached the nosing the step from one tread to
# the next would read 0.18 plus the dish's depth and the facility's worst rise would become this
# stair rather than the great south steps.
let stair_wear_a_1  = ellipsoid 13.38 2.025 3.65 rx=0.10 ry=0.081 rz=0.50
let stair_wear_a_2  = union { stair_wear_a_1  translate { stair_wear_a_1 } -0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_wear_a_4  = union { stair_wear_a_2  translate { stair_wear_a_2 } -0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_wear_a_8  = union { stair_wear_a_4  translate { stair_wear_a_4 } -1.28 0.72 0 }
let stair_wear_a_16 = union { stair_wear_a_8  translate { stair_wear_a_8 } -2.56 1.44 0 }
let stair_wear_a = union { stair_wear_a_16  translate { stair_wear_a_1 } -5.12 2.88 0 }

# --- the spandrel is coursed stone, not one block -----------------------------------------------
#
# Joints 36 mm wide and 36 mm deep on the module's own 0.45, cut into the one face of the spandrel
# anybody can see: the north face, over the well and along the passage. A groove is the hardest
# thin matter there is — it is a feature made entirely of the absence of one voxel — and there are
# seven courses of it running the whole 5.5 m length, each stopping of its own accord where the
# raking face it is cut into runs out from under the flight.
#
# ONLY THAT FACE. The south face is 65 mm inside the halls block and the east face is not a face at
# all — it is the seventeen risers themselves, and a horizontal joint across them would be a joint
# in thin air for all but the bottom 0.18 of its length.
let stair_courses_a = repeat { box 7.95 3.132 4.464   13.60 3.168 4.560 } y=0.45 ny=3

let stair_flight_a = difference {
    union { stair_a_steps stair_nose_a }
    stair_wear_a stair_courses_a
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE HALF LANDING — 1.80 deep (4 M), top 5.04, soffit 4.815
# ================================================================================================
#
# x 6.30 .. 8.20 and the full width of the stair, z 2.875 .. 6.75, bedded 0.15 into the north wall
# and 0.065 into the halls block. It runs 0.10 east of its own face at 8.10 so that the upper
# flight, which starts there, is certainly one solid with it rather than two shapes touching on a
# plane.
#
# 0.225 THICK, WHICH IS M/2 AND WHAT THE ORDER USES FOR A FASCIA, and its soffit at 4.815 is the
# most important number in this file. It clears the arched north window's head at 4.725 by 0.09,
# and it gives the passage below 3.015 m. Both of those are the same decision: the landing had to
# rise until it was out of the window, and once it had, the head height came free.
#
# 1.80 DEEP AND NOT 1.35, and that is the change nobody would have found by looking. You cross a
# half landing between its outer balustrade and the guard over the well. Each of those stands
# 0.2025 in from its own edge — 0.09 of baluster and 0.1125 of handrail — so a 1.35 landing leaves
# 0.945 m to turn round in, which is under a metre in the one place on a stair where somebody is
# carrying something and changing direction. 1.80 leaves 1.395.
#
# THE FACE IS AT 8.10 OVER THE LOWER FLIGHT AND AT 8.20 UNDER THE UPPER ONE. The 0.10 is the
# overlap the upper flight needs so the two are one solid rather than two shapes touching on a
# plane — but carried right across it would stand 0.10 of landing on top of the lower flight's
# seventeenth tread and leave that tread 0.22 deep instead of 0.32, which is a 0.18 step in the
# middle of a stair. So the lip is only where the upper flight buries it.
let stair_land_slab = box 6.315 4.830 2.890   8.085 5.025 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_land_lip  = box 8.015 4.830 4.965   8.185 5.025 6.735 round=0.015

# The landing is PAVED, not cast: joints 36 mm wide and 36 mm deep on the 0.90 (D) square, which is
# the same grid, on the same lines, that the flags of the passage floor below are laid to — so the
# pattern under your feet at 1.80 and the one at 5.04 are one pavement seen twice. A joint in the
# top of a 0.225 slab is a sixth of its depth and takes nothing off what it carries.
let stair_land_j_z = repeat { box 6.30 5.004 4.932   8.20 5.060 4.968 } z=0.90 nz=2
let stair_land_j_x = repeat { box 7.182 5.004 2.87   7.218 5.060 6.75 } x=0.90 nx=1
let stair_landing  = difference {
    union { stair_land_slab stair_land_lip }
    union { stair_land_j_z stair_land_j_x }
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE UPPER FLIGHT — seven risers, 5.04 to 6.30, ascending EAST, a raking slab
# ================================================================================================
#
# Six treads of 0.32 from x = 8.10 to 9.70, the seventh riser being the gallery. This one is
# NOT filled underneath: the passage under it is the point, and its soffit is the surface the
# head-height probe measures against.
#
# The soffit is a plane parallel to the nosing line and 0.405 (0.9 M) below it — a rotated box,
# which is exact and keeps a bounding box where a `plane` written by hand would be neither. Its
# local frame stands on the first nosing at (8.10, 5.22) with local +x running up the rake, and
# it is turned by 0.08154932 turns about z, which is atan(0.18/0.32) / tau. A third box gives the
# flight a VERTICAL west face at x = 8.04 — 0.06 west of the first riser, so that the first tread
# keeps the nosing it overhangs the landing with; without it the rotated box's own end cuts the
# first riser on the slant and the stair starts with a wedge.
#
#   tread  1   x  8.10   top 5.22        tread  4   x  9.06   top 5.76
#   tread  2   x  8.42   top 5.40        tread  5   x  9.38   top 5.94
#   tread  3   x  8.74   top 5.58        tread  6   x  9.70   top 6.12
#                                        gallery    x 10.02   top 6.30
#
# Tread six runs on to 10.07 so that it is buried in the gallery, whose face at 10.02 is the
# seventh riser.
#
# THE NESTING GOES THE OTHER WAY ROUND FROM THE LOWER FLIGHT, and getting that wrong is silent.
# A nested-box stair is a stack of boxes each of which runs from its own riser AWAY IN THE
# DIRECTION OF DESCENT, so that every box below is buried in the ones above. The lower flight
# climbs west, so its boxes all end at the same eastern edge; this one climbs east, so its boxes
# all start at the same western edge. Written the lower flight's way the tallest box covers all
# the others and the flight comes back as a SOLID BLOCK with a flat top. One component, no
# floating voxels, no error, and a plausible voxel count. The head-height probe is what found it.
let stair_b_01 = box  8.115 4.200 4.965   10.055 5.205 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_02 = box  8.435 4.200 4.965   10.055 5.385 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_03 = box  8.755 4.200 4.965   10.055 5.565 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_04 = box  9.075 4.200 4.965   10.055 5.745 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_05 = box  9.395 4.200 4.965   10.055 5.925 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_06 = box  9.715 4.200 4.965   10.055 6.105 6.735 round=0.015

let stair_b_steps = union {
    stair_b_01 stair_b_02 stair_b_03 stair_b_04 stair_b_05 stair_b_06
}

# The nosings of this flight overhang WESTWARD, because this flight climbs east — the overhang is
# always over the riser you have just come up. The first one hangs 0.045 over the half landing,
# which is what a nosing at the foot of a flight does.
let stair_nose_b_bar  = box  8.070 5.157 4.965    8.145 5.205 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_nose_b_cove = cavetto 8.100 5.142 4.950   8.055 5.085 6.750 run=z
let stair_nose_b_1    = union { stair_nose_b_bar stair_nose_b_cove }
let stair_nose_b_2    = union { stair_nose_b_1  translate { stair_nose_b_1 } 0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_nose_b_4    = union { stair_nose_b_2  translate { stair_nose_b_2 } 0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_nose_b      = union { stair_nose_b_4  translate { stair_nose_b_2 } 1.28 0.72 0 }

let stair_wear_b_1 = ellipsoid 8.26 5.265 5.85 rx=0.10 ry=0.081 rz=0.52
let stair_wear_b_2 = union { stair_wear_b_1  translate { stair_wear_b_1 } 0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_wear_b_4 = union { stair_wear_b_2  translate { stair_wear_b_2 } 0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_wear_b   = union { stair_wear_b_4  translate { stair_wear_b_2 } 1.28 0.72 0 }

# THE SOFFIT PLANE IS PLACED BY A POINT ON IT, not by an offset below the nosing line, and the
# difference is 60 mm of head height. `rotate` turns the box about the origin, so a box whose
# bottom face sits 0.405 BELOW the local origin has that face 0.405 away PERPENDICULAR to the
# rake — which is 0.405 / cos(29.4 degrees) = 0.465 straight down. Written this way — the box's
# bottom face ON the local origin, and the origin carried to the soffit's own corner at
# (8.10, 4.815) — the waist is 0.405 VERTICALLY at every point of the flight, because the soffit
# and the nosing line are parallel and 0.405 apart in y by construction rather than by trigonometry.
#
# THE WAIST BOX IS 15 mm WIDER THAN THE FLIGHT IN z ON PURPOSE. Written to the flight's own
# 4.965..6.735 it clips the treads back to those numbers instead of to the 4.95..6.75 their own
# `round` gives them, and the flight comes out 30 mm narrower than every number in this file says.
let stair_b_waist_sec = box -1.00 0.00 4.940   4.30 3.00 6.760
let stair_b_waist = translate { rotate { stair_b_waist_sec } z=0.08154932 } 8.10 4.815 0
let stair_b_face  = box 8.04 3.00 4.95   10.10 7.00 6.75

let stair_flight_b = difference {
    intersection { union { stair_b_steps stair_nose_b } stair_b_waist stair_b_face }
    stair_wear_b
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE GALLERY — the upper floor this stair arrives at, top 6.30, soffit 5.895
# ================================================================================================
#
# x 10.02 .. 15.25 by z 4.50 .. 6.75: 5.08 by 2.10 of walkable upper floor, bedded 0.15 into the
# east wall and 0.15 into the north wall. Its top is the piano nobile's own window sill, which is
# how you know 6.30 is right: 1.80 plus twenty-five risers of 0.18 lands on the level windows.clip
# already put its upper sills at.
#
# It is 0.405 thick, the flight's own waist, so the two soffits are one continuous plane and the
# joint between them at x = 10.02 has no step in it at all. Paved to the same 0.90 (D) grid as the
# half landing and the floor below, on the same lines.
#
# WHEN SOMEBODY BUILDS THE REST OF THE UPPER FLOOR it butts this: the open edges are z = 4.50
# from x 10.02 to 15.10, and x = 10.02 from z 4.50 to 4.95. Both carry a balustrade now because
# a landing over a 4.5 m drop has to; take the first out when there is a floor on the other side
# of it, and LEAVE THE SECOND — it is over the open well.
let stair_gall_slab = box 10.035 5.910 4.515   15.235 6.285 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_gall_j_x  = repeat { box 13.332 6.264 4.50   13.368 6.320 6.75 } x=0.90 nx=4
let stair_gall_j_z  = repeat { box 10.02 6.264 4.932   15.25 6.320 4.968 } z=0.90 nz=2
let stair_gallery   = difference { stair_gall_slab  union { stair_gall_j_x stair_gall_j_z } }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PIERS — what carries the landing and the gallery over the passage
# ================================================================================================
#
# Square, 0.45 (M), founded at 1.75 and running up 0.03 into the slab above so nothing is left
# resting on a plane. Two under the west edge of the half landing, two under the gallery, all four
# on the same 0.45 grid, and NONE OF THEM LESS THAN 1.20 m FROM ITS NEIGHBOUR: you walk between
# these, and a pair 0.93 apart is a pair you turn sideways for. The landing's two stand 2.91 apart
# and the gallery's 1.35, and each pair is set against a wall at one end — the halls block at 2.940
# and the north wall at 6.60 — so the gaps are where a person is rather than beside them.
#
# THEY HAVE NO PLINTH AND NO CAP AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, TWICE OVER. A cap projecting 0.045 under
# a soffit is a ring of stone at head height over a floor people walk on; and a plinth 0.225 tall
# beside a floor is a 0.225 rise in a building whose worst rise is supposed to be the steps' 0.18,
# and measure.cpp counts any rise up to 0.75 m as a step somebody meant to take. So the ornament is
# SUNK instead: a panel in each face, which costs nothing in either check and gives the passage
# something to be lit by.
#
# FOUR CUTTERS AND NOT TWO, ONE TO A FACE, EACH 0.09 DEEP. Two cutters — one across x and one
# across z, each run past both faces — is the obvious way to sink a panel on all four sides of a
# pier and it does not sink anything: each one goes straight THROUGH, and their union takes the
# whole middle out and leaves four corner posts.
#
# 0.09 (M/5) DEEP AND NOT 0.045, and the reason is paint rather than shadow. A rule keyed on a
# recess has to name a zone that holds the recess floor and excludes the face 45 mm outside it,
# and the rules' own `below=0.02` reaches 20 mm each way off a voxel centre that is itself up to
# 16 mm off the surface. There is no room in 45 mm; there is in 90. halls.clip found the same
# thing from the other side, so this is the building's second vote for the same number.
let stair_pier_body = box  6.315 1.765 2.955    6.735 4.830 3.375 round=0.015
let stair_pier_pan_s = box 6.390 2.25 2.850    6.660 4.35 3.030
let stair_pier_pan_n = box 6.390 2.25 3.300    6.660 4.35 3.480
let stair_pier_pan_w = box 6.210 2.25 3.030    6.390 4.35 3.300
let stair_pier_pan_e = box 6.660 2.25 3.030    6.840 4.35 3.300
let stair_pier_one = difference {
    stair_pier_body
    union { stair_pier_pan_s stair_pier_pan_n stair_pier_pan_w stair_pier_pan_e }
}

let stair_pier_a = stair_pier_one
let stair_pier_b = translate { stair_pier_one } 0 0 3.36

let stair_tpier_body  = box 11.625 1.765 4.515   12.045 5.910 4.935 round=0.015
let stair_tpier_pan_s = box 11.700 2.25 4.410   11.970 5.40 4.590
let stair_tpier_pan_n = box 11.700 2.25 4.860   11.970 5.40 5.040
let stair_tpier_pan_w = box 11.520 2.25 4.590   11.700 5.40 4.860
let stair_tpier_pan_e = box 11.970 2.25 4.590   12.150 5.40 4.860
let stair_tpier_one = difference {
    stair_tpier_body
    union { stair_tpier_pan_s stair_tpier_pan_n stair_tpier_pan_w stair_tpier_pan_e }
}

let stair_pier_c = stair_tpier_one
let stair_pier_d = translate { stair_tpier_one } 0 0 1.80

let stair_piers = union { stair_pier_a stair_pier_b stair_pier_c stair_pier_d }

# ================================================================================================
# THE BALUSTRADE — the order's turned baluster, one to a tread, on a bronze shoe
# ================================================================================================
#
# `ionic_baluster` is 0.70 tall on a plinth 0.18 square, which is exactly one run less 0.14, so
# one to a tread at 0.32 centres leaves a 0.14 gap and a 0.063 neck. The handrail over it is
# 0.225 tall, so the rail crown stands 0.925 above the surface a baluster stands on.
#
# THE SHOE AND THE COLLAR ARE THE FIXINGS, and they are matter rather than paint because at 36 mm
# and 18 mm proud they are both bigger than a voxel at the contract's metre. A stone baluster is
# not glued to its string: it is doweled, and the dowel is hidden under a metal shoe that spreads
# the load and keeps the water out of the joint, with a matching collar where the neck enters the
# rail. The shoe stands 0.018 proud of the plinth it wraps and the collar 0.024 proud of the rail's
# own fillet, and BOTH STAY INSIDE THE 0.1125 HALF-WIDTH OF THE HANDRAIL ABOVE THEM — 0.108 and
# 0.099 — so neither adds a millimetre to what the flight costs in width and neither can be the
# topmost matter in its own column, which is what would have made either of them a rise. At 0.216
# across on 0.32 centres the shoes leave a 0.104 gap between neighbours and do not run together.
let stair_bal_shoe   = box -0.098 0.010 -0.098   0.098 0.026 0.098 round=0.010
let stair_bal_collar = box -0.089 0.674 -0.089   0.089 0.690 0.089 round=0.010
let stair_baluster   = union { ionic_baluster stair_bal_shoe stair_bal_collar }
#
# 0.925 IS NOT A COMFORT DECISION. measure.cpp reports the worst rise between neighbouring columns
# and treats anything over 0.75 m as a wall rather than as a bad step. A rail 0.70 above its tread
# — a baluster with no rail on it — puts a 0.70 rise beside every tread in this stair and makes
# THAT the number the whole facility reports instead of the great steps' 0.18. The continuous
# rail is what saves it: every column along the balustrade has rail over it, and the LOWEST that
# rail ever reads is 0.835 (see the handrail section for where that number comes from), clear of
# the cutoff. Nothing between a tread and its rail is allowed to top out between 0.19 and 0.75
# above it, which is why the balusters carry no separate plinth of their own and why the raking
# plinth below sits exactly one riser and no more above the tread beside it.
#
# THE RAKING RUNS ARE PLACED BY DOUBLING. `repeat` steps along one axis; a stair balustrade steps
# along two at once, and the balusters must stay upright, so a rotated repeat is no use either.
# One baluster, unioned with itself carried one tread up the rake, gives two; that with itself
# carried two treads gives four, then eight, then sixteen. Seventeen is sixteen and one; six is
# four and two. Five lines for seventeen balusters, and every one of them is a translate of the
# same shape.
#
# AND THEY STAND ON THE NOSING LINE, NOT ON THE TREADS. A rail is a fixed height over the line
# the nosings make; a tread is level and the line is not, so a baluster standing on the middle of
# a tread is 0.09 short of a rail that is straight, and there is a 90 mm slot the length of the
# balustrade. What fixes it is the RAKING PLINTH below: a band 0.09 (M/5) deep whose top face IS
# the nosing line, filling the saw-tooth between the line and the treads, so every baluster stands
# on one plane and the doubling's own step of one tread up and one run along is exactly the
# plinth's own slope.
let stair_ra_1  = translate { stair_baluster } 13.54 1.98 4.41
let stair_ra_2  = union { stair_ra_1  translate { stair_ra_1 } -0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_ra_4  = union { stair_ra_2  translate { stair_ra_2 } -0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_ra_8  = union { stair_ra_4  translate { stair_ra_4 } -1.28 0.72 0 }
let stair_ra_16 = union { stair_ra_8  translate { stair_ra_8 } -2.56 1.44 0 }
let stair_bal_a = union { stair_ra_16 translate { stair_ra_1 } -5.12 2.88 0 }

let stair_rb_1  = translate { stair_baluster } 8.10 5.22 5.04
let stair_rb_2  = union { stair_rb_1  translate { stair_rb_1 } 0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_rb_4  = union { stair_rb_2  translate { stair_rb_2 } 0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_bal_b = union { stair_rb_4  translate { stair_rb_2 } 1.28 0.72 0 }

# The level runs: the landing's west edge, both ends of the well, and the gallery's south and west
# edges. Same 0.32 pitch as the flights, so the whole balustrade is one rhythm. THE LANDING'S
# SOUTH EDGE HAS NONE, and neither has the upper flight's north side or the gallery's: those are
# against walls now, which is the whole reason this stair is wide enough to walk on.
let stair_bal_lw    = translate { repeat { translate { stair_baluster } 6.39 5.04 0 } z=0.32 nz=5 } 0 0 4.77
let stair_bal_lwell = translate { stair_baluster } 8.01 5.04 4.725
let stair_bal_gs    = translate { repeat { translate { stair_baluster } 0 6.30 4.59 } x=0.32 nx=7 } 12.56 0 0
let stair_bal_gwell = translate { stair_baluster } 10.11 6.30 4.725

let stair_balusters = union {
    stair_bal_a stair_bal_b
    stair_bal_lw stair_bal_lwell stair_bal_gs stair_bal_gwell
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE HANDRAIL — a moulded section, 0.225 wide (M/2) and 0.225 tall (M/2)
# ================================================================================================
#
#   -0.225 .. -0.180   fillet   M/10 tall, 0.15 wide, the band the balusters die into
#   -0.180 .. -0.090   core     M/5 tall, 0.12 wide, the plain neck
#   -0.090 ..  0.000   ovolo    M/5, a quarter round of 0.1125 either side — THE WIDEST MEMBER,
#                               and the only one 0.225 across, so it is what every column finds
#
# measured DOWN from the rail top, because that is the surface a hand takes and the only one that
# has to be a fixed height over a tread. Two ovolos rather than a `mirror`: mirror folds to the
# absolute value of a coordinate, so a mirrored section has to be built at zero and carried, and
# both of these rails are already being built at zero for the rotation.
#
# BOTH OF THOSE NUMBERS ARE SET BY THE WALKABILITY CHECK AND NOT BY A HAND. measure.cpp looks down
# EVERY COLUMN of voxels; the column that grazes the very edge of a rail does not find the crown,
# it finds whichever member is widest there. Built 0.18 tall with the fillet the widest, that edge
# column topped out 0.7375 above the landing beside it — under the 0.75 the check treats as a step
# somebody meant to take, so this handrail, and not the great south steps, was the worst rise in
# the entire facility. It reported as "0.75 m" and it took decoding a voxel index to find.
#
# So the rail is M/2 tall and THE OVOLO IS THE WIDEST MEMBER — 0.225 across against the fillet's
# 0.15 and the core's 0.12. Widening the fillet to match was tried first and is not enough: the
# ovolo is a quarter round, so at the very edge of its 0.1125 it has no height at all, and the
# outermost sampled column found the fillet under it again at 0.77. With the fillet pulled in,
# the shallowest top any column under this rail can have is the ovolo's own springing,
# 0.925 - 0.09 = 0.835 above the surface, clear of the cutoff by 0.085 — nearly three voxels at
# metre 32.
#
# THE TWO RAKING RAILS are one straight section built about the origin and turned about z by the
# stair's own slope. The lower flight descends toward +x and the upper one climbs toward +x, so
# they are the SAME section turned opposite ways, which is the check that the angle is right: if
# one of them is parallel to its nosings the other is not.
#
# Both lengths start at local x = 0, which is the HEAD of the lower flight and the FOOT of the
# upper one, and both run INTO the newel that terminates them and no further: 6.25 along the rake
# from (8.10, 5.965) arrives at (13.548, 2.901) against a newel top of 2.905, and 2.21 from
# (8.10, 6.145) arrives at (10.026, 7.229) against a newel top of 7.225. Both are inside the
# newel's own 0.2010, and neither is measured to the newel's CENTRE — that is the mistake the old
# file made at both ends. A rail is 0.925 over its own nosing line and the nosing line keeps
# climbing past the last riser, so a rail run to the middle of a newel standing on the floor above
# arrives 0.08 higher than the newel does and leaves a spike. Run it to where the two heights
# agree, which is the edge the newel stands 0.09 inside of, and it dies inside the newel instead.
let stair_rail_a_fil = fillet 0.00 -0.225 -0.075  6.25 -0.18  0.075 run=x
let stair_rail_a_cor = box    0.00 -0.18  -0.06   6.25 -0.09  0.06  round=0.012
let stair_rail_a_ovl = ovolo  0.00 -0.09   0.00   6.25  0.00  0.1125 run=x
let stair_rail_a_ovr = ovolo  0.00 -0.09   0.00   6.25  0.00 -0.1125 run=x
let stair_rail_a_sec = union {
    stair_rail_a_fil stair_rail_a_cor stair_rail_a_ovl stair_rail_a_ovr
}

let stair_rail_b_fil = fillet 0.00 -0.225 -0.075  2.21 -0.18  0.075 run=x
let stair_rail_b_cor = box    0.00 -0.18  -0.06   2.21 -0.09  0.06  round=0.012
let stair_rail_b_ovl = ovolo  0.00 -0.09   0.00   2.21  0.00  0.1125 run=x
let stair_rail_b_ovr = ovolo  0.00 -0.09   0.00   2.21  0.00 -0.1125 run=x
let stair_rail_b_sec = union {
    stair_rail_b_fil stair_rail_b_cor stair_rail_b_ovl stair_rail_b_ovr
}

let stair_rail_down = rotate { stair_rail_a_sec } z=-0.08154932
let stair_rail_up   = rotate { stair_rail_b_sec } z=0.08154932

let stair_rail_a = translate { stair_rail_down } 8.10 5.965 4.41
let stair_rail_b = translate { stair_rail_up }   8.10 6.145 5.04

# The raking plinths, in the same two frames but with local y = 0 on the NOSING LINE rather than
# on the rail top, which is the only datum a balustrade on a stair has.
let stair_plin_a_sec = fillet 0.00 -0.09 -0.09   6.25 0.00 0.09 run=x
let stair_plin_b_sec = fillet 0.00 -0.09 -0.09   2.21 0.00 0.09 run=x
let stair_plin_down = rotate { stair_plin_a_sec } z=-0.08154932
let stair_plin_up   = rotate { stair_plin_b_sec } z=0.08154932

let stair_plinths = union {
    translate { stair_plin_down } 8.10 5.04 4.41
    translate { stair_plin_up }   8.10 5.22 5.04
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE WALL STRINGS — the raking skirting on the side of each flight that is a wall
# ================================================================================================
#
# The two sides of this stair that used to carry a balustrade and now carry a wall still have to be
# finished, and a flight dying into bare plaster is the one thing that tells you a stair was drawn
# rather than built. A close string is a raking skirting: a band whose top runs 0.225 (M/2) above
# the nosing line and whose foot dies 0.09 below it, standing 0.045 (M/10) proud of the wall, with
# a quarter round rolling its top back into the plaster.
#
# 0.045 AND NOT 0.09, AND THE REASON IS THE WIDTH RATHER THAN THE LOOK. Everything that stands on
# a wall along a walking route is subtracted from the route. 0.045 is inside the same voxel as the
# wall face it stands on at metre 8 and at metre 16, so this member costs the flight NOTHING that
# any resolution can measure, where a hand-height rail at 0.09 would have cost 90 mm of a clearance
# with 157 mm to give. There is a handrail on this stair and it is on the well, where a hand can
# reach it without standing in the way.
#
# The section is read in (z, y) because these run along x. The ovolo's first corner is the one the
# curve is FULL at — build_moulding centres its quarter disc there — so it is written at the bottom
# of the capping against the wall and the second corner out at the string's own face.
#
# BOTH ARE CLIPPED, and both clips are load-bearing. A rotated box does not end where its length
# says it does: the end face is perpendicular to the rake, so a section 0.315 tall turned 29.36
# degrees reaches 0.154 further along x than the axis does. Unclipped, the lower string put a 45 mm
# tail of skirting through the floor and past the foot of the stair, and the probe found it as a
# single voxel. The upper one is capped at 6.28 because a skirting 0.225 over its nosing line
# reaches 6.525 by the time it arrives at the gallery, and the piano nobile's north windows are cut
# y 6.30..8.55 at x 10.125..11.475 — capped, it dies flat into the underside of the floor above,
# which is what a skirting does at the head of a flight anyway.
let stair_string_a_sec = union {
    fillet 0.00 -0.090 0.000   6.45 0.180 0.045 run=x
    ovolo  0.00  0.180 0.000   6.45 0.225 0.045 run=x
}
let stair_string_b_sec = union {
    fillet 0.00 -0.090 0.000   2.21 0.180 -0.045 run=x
    ovolo  0.00  0.180 0.000   2.21 0.225 -0.045 run=x
}
let stair_string_a = intersection {
    translate { rotate { stair_string_a_sec } z=-0.08154932 } 8.10 5.04 2.940
    box 8.00 1.80 2.90   13.72 5.60 3.00
}
let stair_string_b = intersection {
    translate { rotate { stair_string_b_sec } z=0.08154932 } 8.10 5.22 6.600
    box 8.00 3.00 6.50   10.15 6.28 6.66
}

# The level length of it round the half landing, which is the same section standing still. It stops
# at 8.10 rather than running on under the upper flight, because past there the wall is behind a
# flight and not beside a floor.
let stair_string_land = union {
    fillet 6.30 4.950 2.940   8.10 5.220 2.985 run=x
    ovolo  6.30 5.220 2.940   8.10 5.265 2.985 run=x
}
let stair_strings = union { stair_string_a stair_string_b stair_string_land }

# The level rails. Built where they stand, so the two ovolos are written either side of the run
# rather than folded. `run=x` reads the section in (z, y); the default `run=z` reads it in (x, y).
# The landing's rails are 0.925 over 5.04 and the gallery's 0.925 over 6.30.
let stair_rail_lw = union {
    fillet 6.315 5.740 2.94   6.465 5.785 6.60
    box    6.33  5.785 2.94   6.45  5.875 6.60 round=0.012
    ovolo  6.39  5.875 2.94   6.5025 5.965 6.60
    ovolo  6.39  5.875 2.94   6.2775 5.965 6.60
}
let stair_rail_lwell = union {
    fillet 7.935 5.740 4.41   8.085 5.785 5.04
    box    7.95  5.785 4.41   8.07  5.875 5.04 round=0.012
    ovolo  8.01  5.875 4.41   8.1225 5.965 5.04
    ovolo  8.01  5.875 4.41   7.8975 5.965 5.04
}
let stair_rail_gs = union {
    fillet 10.02 7.000 4.515  15.10 7.045 4.665 run=x
    box    10.02 7.045 4.53   15.10 7.135 4.65  round=0.012
    ovolo  10.02 7.135 4.59   15.10 7.225 4.7025 run=x
    ovolo  10.02 7.135 4.59   15.10 7.225 4.4775 run=x
}
let stair_rail_gwell = union {
    fillet 10.035 7.000 4.59   10.185 7.045 5.04
    box    10.05  7.045 4.59   10.17  7.135 5.04 round=0.012
    ovolo  10.11  7.135 4.59   10.2225 7.225 5.04
    ovolo  10.11  7.135 4.59    9.9975 7.225 5.04
}

let stair_rails = union {
    stair_rail_a stair_rail_b
    stair_rail_lw stair_rail_lwell stair_rail_gs stair_rail_gwell
}

# THE NEWELS. 0.225 square (M/2), from the surface they stand on to the rail top and NOT ONE
# MILLIMETRE HIGHER — a finial standing 0.18 over a rail is a 0.18 rise beside it, which is the
# building's own riser and would tie the great steps for the worst rise in the facility. Flush is
# free; see the pier note above for the same arithmetic.
# Written 0.012 inside its own faces so the round puts them back, for the reason given over the
# lower flight's treads: a newel 12 mm proud of the rail it terminates is a 7-voxel rise beside a
# building whose worst step is meant to be 6. 0.225 square is exactly the rail's own widest member.
let stair_newel = box -0.1005 -0.018 -0.1005   0.1005 0.913 0.1005 round=0.012

# The one at the foot stands on the FLOOR and not on a tread, and the floor is one riser below the
# first nosing, so it is one riser taller. Both numbers are the rail's: 0.925 over the surface the
# rail is 0.925 over, 1.105 over the one it is 1.105 over. A newel that stops short of its rail is
# a 0.18 rise beside the rail, which is the thing this whole balustrade is arranged to avoid. IT
# STANDS ON THE FIRST RISER'S OWN LINE at x = 13.54, and the old one did not: it stood 0.18 further
# out, where the rail it was meant to finish had already descended another 0.10, and it was 0.10
# proud of it for the whole life of the file.
let stair_newel_foot = box -0.1005 -0.018 -0.1005   0.1005 1.093 0.1005 round=0.012

# The one at the head of the well, at (8.01, 5.04), is the tall one too, and for the same reason
# in reverse: the raking rail leaves the landing 0.18 higher than the level rail arrives at it,
# because a rail is a fixed height over its own nosing and the first nosing is one riser up. The
# newel is what absorbs that, which is what a newel is for.
let stair_newels = union {
    translate { stair_newel_foot } 13.54 1.80 4.41
    translate { stair_newel }       8.01 5.04 4.41
    translate { stair_newel_foot }  8.01 5.04 5.04
    translate { stair_newel }       6.39 5.04 3.02
    translate { stair_newel }       6.39 5.04 6.52
    translate { stair_newel }      10.11 6.30 4.59
    translate { stair_newel }      10.11 6.30 5.04
    translate { stair_newel }      15.01 6.30 4.59
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let stair_east = union {
    stair_flight_a stair_landing stair_flight_b stair_gallery
    stair_piers stair_plinths stair_strings stair_balusters stair_rails stair_newels
}

let part_stair = mirror { stair_east } axis=x

# What this part removes from the building: the joints between the flags of the floor it stands on,
# and nothing else. It is bedded INTO its neighbours rather than cut out of them, so it needs no
# room made for it — but the passage under this stair is 24 m2 of floor lit only by bounce, and a
# floor with no joints in it is one surface pretending to be a pavement. The grid is 0.90 (D)
# square, the joints are 36 mm wide and cut 42 mm into the podium, and the whole thing is caught in
# a box so a `repeat` cannot wander into the rotunda's wall.
let stair_fl_j_x = repeat { box 6.15 1.758 4.932   15.30 1.815 4.968 } z=0.90 nz=2
let stair_fl_j_z = repeat { box 10.632 1.758 2.94   10.668 1.815 6.75 } x=0.90 nx=5
let stair_fl_joints = intersection {
    union { stair_fl_j_x stair_fl_j_z }
    box 6.15 1.70 2.955   15.30 1.90 6.735
}
let void_stair = mirror { stair_fl_joints } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE HEAD-HEIGHT PROBE — the measurement this part exists to make
# ================================================================================================
#
# Not part of the building. `stair_probe_head` is the volume a person sweeps through every place
# this stair lets them stand, INTERSECTED with the stair. Run it and it must be empty:
#
#     --part stair_probe_head        -> "the clip is empty"
#
# Five volumes. Every one of them is 2.40 m tall — the circulation figure and not the 2.10 an
# opening is allowed — and every one of them is AT LEAST 1.20 m WIDE between the members that
# stand beside it, which is the half of this check that did not exist before and the half that
# found the real faults:
#
#   pass    the passage under the stair: 1.80 to 4.20 over the whole footprint. This is the one
#           that matters. The lower flight's spandrel, its wall string and the four piers are solid
#           from the floor and are taken out of it, because you walk round a pier and under a
#           soffit.
#   rake_a  2.40 over each of the seventeen lower treads, 1.20 wide between the wall string and the
#           balustrade, and inset 0.06 from the nosing above so that a nosing at ankle height is
#           not reported as a low ceiling.
#   rake_b  2.40 over each of the six upper treads, 1.30 wide.
#   land    2.40 over the half landing, in two boxes: 1.34 wide the length of it and 1.57 wide
#           where you step off the lower flight, past the guard over the well.
#   gall    2.40 over the gallery, 1.79 wide, and 1.35 where you arrive off the upper flight past
#           the newel at the head of the well.
#
# WHAT THE PROBE CANNOT SEE, so that nobody trusts it further than it goes: it tests THIS part
# against ITSELF. Something another fragment hangs over the passage later will not show up here,
# and neither will a window cutter — the arched opening that set this stair's riser split was found
# by reading windows.clip's own numbers and checking them against every member in this file by
# hand, not by any probe.

# WHAT COMES OUT OF THE PASSAGE VOLUME, and why it is a plan footprint and not the shapes. You
# walk ROUND a pier and UNDER a soffit, so a pier is not a head-height failure — but nor is the
# handrail two metres up in the air over the lower flight, and that stands inside the passage's
# box just as much as the pier does. So what is subtracted is the GROUND PLAN of everything that
# rises off the floor: the lower flight with its balustrade, its skirting and its foot newel, and
# the four piers. A shape would have left the balusters standing in the probe and reported them as
# low ceilings, which is exactly what the first version of this file did.
let stair_probe_box   = box 6.15 1.80 2.955   15.30 4.20 6.585
let stair_probe_pl_a  = box 7.95 1.70 2.85   13.75 4.30 4.56
let stair_probe_pl_p1 = box 6.25 1.70 2.89    6.80 4.30 3.44
let stair_probe_pl_p2 = box 6.25 1.70 6.25    6.80 4.30 6.80
let stair_probe_pl_p3 = box 11.56 1.70 4.45  12.11 4.30 5.00
let stair_probe_pl_p4 = box 11.56 1.70 6.25  12.11 4.30 6.80
let stair_probe_stand = union {
    stair_probe_pl_a stair_probe_pl_p1 stair_probe_pl_p2 stair_probe_pl_p3 stair_probe_pl_p4
}
let stair_probe_pass = difference { stair_probe_box stair_probe_stand }

# THE VOLUME OVER A FLIGHT IS SEVENTEEN AND SIX UPRIGHT BOXES, ONE TO A TREAD, and not one box
# turned by the pitch. A turned box was the first thing tried, because that is how a stair is
# drawn on paper: a rectangle standing on the nosing line. It is the wrong instrument. Head height
# is measured VERTICALLY from the tread you are standing on; the rotated box measures it
# perpendicular to the pitch, which is 13% short, and its lower face lies exactly along the
# nosings, so every nosing in the flight pokes into it and it reports a failure the whole length
# of a stair that has nothing over it at all. One box per tread, from the tread top to 2.40 above
# it, is the thing the rule actually says, and it is placed by the same doubling the balusters are.
let stair_pra_1  = box 13.265 1.98 3.045   13.54 4.38 4.245
let stair_pra_2  = union { stair_pra_1  translate { stair_pra_1 } -0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_pra_4  = union { stair_pra_2  translate { stair_pra_2 } -0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_pra_8  = union { stair_pra_4  translate { stair_pra_4 } -1.28 0.72 0 }
let stair_pra_16 = union { stair_pra_8  translate { stair_pra_8 } -2.56 1.44 0 }
let stair_probe_ra = union { stair_pra_16  translate { stair_pra_1 } -5.12 2.88 0 }

let stair_prb_1  = box 8.10 5.22 5.20   8.36 7.62 6.50
let stair_prb_2  = union { stair_prb_1  translate { stair_prb_1 } 0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_prb_4  = union { stair_prb_2  translate { stair_prb_2 } 0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_probe_rb = union { stair_prb_4  translate { stair_prb_2 } 1.28 0.72 0 }

let stair_probe_land = union {
    box 6.53 5.04 3.045   7.87 7.44 6.50
    box 6.53 5.04 3.045   8.10 7.44 4.28
}
let stair_probe_gall = union {
    box 11.10 6.30 4.73   15.07 8.70 6.52
    box 10.26 6.30 5.17   15.07 8.70 6.52
}

# The five, each on its own so a failure can be found rather than only seen.
let stair_probe_p_pass = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_pass }
let stair_probe_p_ra   = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_ra }
let stair_probe_p_rb   = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_rb }
let stair_probe_p_land = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_land }
let stair_probe_p_gall = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_gall }

let stair_probe_all = union {
    stair_probe_pass stair_probe_ra stair_probe_rb stair_probe_land stair_probe_gall
}
let stair_probe_head = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_all }

# AND THE ONE PROBE THAT LOOKS OUTSIDE THIS FILE, because the fault it catches cannot be seen from
# inside it. `void_windows` is what the manifest subtracts from the whole building, and anything of
# mine inside it is deleted silently — no error, no floating voxels, no change in the component
# count, just a hole. The arched north opening at x = +-8.10 was taking a 0.68 x 1.05 m bite out of
# the half landing, in both wings, for the whole life of this file, and nothing in it could tell.
#
#     --part stair_probe_cut        -> "the clip is empty"
#
# It names a binding another fragment owns, which BRIEF.md rule 2 is against and which is the right
# trade here twice over: `void_windows` is already named by the manifest, so it cannot be renamed
# without breaking that first and loudly; and a fragment that stands inside somebody else's cutter
# has no other way to find out.
let stair_probe_cut = intersection { stair_east void_windows }

# ================================================================================================
# THE MEASURING RIG — how the head heights in this file's notes were actually taken
# ================================================================================================
#
# `--gap y@x,z` on the whole facility answers a different question from the one it looks like it
# answers. It reports the LONGEST unbroken run of air down a column, and over any point in this
# passage the longest run of air is not the passage: it is the metres between the gallery and the
# roof, or the open sky above the parapet. BRIEF.md says to use a probe clip whose box is one
# storey tall, and a fragment cannot — its box is the whole building's.
#
# So the rig is this stair with the four surfaces that bound it put back and nothing else: the
# floor at 1.80 (podium_top), the halls block's face at 2.940, the north wall's inner face at 6.60,
# and a lid at 7.50. The floor, the walls and the lid all run to the ends of the clip so that no
# run outside this storey can ever be the longest one, and the numbers then mean what they say:
#
#     bash tools/clipcheck.sh clips/facility.clip --part stair_rig --metre 8 --gap y@7.40,5.50
#
# NOTHING HERE IS PART OF THE BUILDING and the lid is not a ceiling — it is the far end of a ruler.
# A run that touches it is a reading of the rig; only the runs under a slab of this stair are
# readings of the stair.
# EVERY ONE OF THESE RUNS TO THE END OF THE SAMPLED ARRAY and that is not belt and braces. The
# sampler settles the box to the SHAPE and not to the `bounds` line, so a floor drawn to y = -1.00
# leaves 3.50 m of array under it, and `longest_clear` finds that instead — it reported "3.500 m,
# OPEN at the near end" on a passage 3.015 m high, which is a plausible number and the wrong one.
# A ruler has to be longer than the thing it measures in every direction it does not measure.
let stair_rig_floor = box   6.15 -12.00 -16.50   15.30  1.80  8.50
let stair_rig_south = box   6.15 -12.00 -16.50   15.30 25.00  2.940
let stair_rig_north = box   6.15 -12.00   6.600  15.30 25.00  8.50
let stair_rig_lid   = box   6.15   7.50   2.940  15.30 25.00  6.600
let stair_rig_west  = box -17.50 -12.00 -16.50    6.15 25.00  8.50
let stair_rig_east  = box  15.30 -12.00 -16.50   17.50 25.00  8.50
let stair_rig = union {
    stair_east stair_rig_floor stair_rig_south stair_rig_north stair_rig_lid
    stair_rig_west stair_rig_east
}

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# A grand stair is not one stone. Granite where it is founded and where a foot lands hardest,
# marble for everything the eye follows up — treads, landings, balusters, newels, strings — and
# BRONZE on the handrail and on every baluster's shoe and collar, because the contract says metal
# where the hand touches it and because a brushed metal rail 22 m long, raking through a room lit
# only by bounce, is a fairer question for a path tracer than any flat panel of it. Porphyry in
# the sunk panels of the piers, which is the only saturated colour down in the passage.
#
# Every rule keys on one of this file's own shapes, folded to both wings so the west stair is
# painted by the same rules as the east and cannot drift, and every one says below=0.02: the
# manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm of grain at the end, and a test at zero misses
# the voxels the displacement made and leaves a rind of limestone over the part that painted it.
# The rule keyed on a shape a `rotate` put where it is says 0.035, which is BRIEF.md rule 5:
# a rotated surface lies nowhere near a voxel boundary, so between 3 and 16 per cent of its own
# solid voxels fall outside its own test at 0.02 and come back wearing the base coat.
#
# NO WEATHERING, and that is a decision. `weather` displaces a surface, and the two surfaces this
# fragment is judged on are the treads, which carry the facility's worst-rise number, and the
# soffits, which carry its head height. A 14 mm desert coat moves both by an amount no render
# would tell you about. The wear in the treads is geometry, written down, and repeatable; noise
# would be neither. Inside stone stays clean.

let stair_granite = mirror { union { stair_flight_a stair_piers } } axis=x
paint granite where=stair_granite below=0.02

let stair_marble = mirror { union { stair_landing stair_flight_b stair_gallery
                                    stair_plinths stair_strings
                                    stair_balusters stair_newels } } axis=x
paint marble where=stair_marble below=0.02

let stair_bronze = mirror { stair_rails } axis=x
paint bronze where=stair_bronze below=0.02

# THE SHOES AND COLLARS, which are the one place in this file a rule has to find a band of a shape
# rather than a shape. They are unioned into `stair_baluster` itself so that the doubling carries
# them — writing them as a second doubling would be forty more lines of the same arithmetic and a
# second place to get it wrong — so what marks them out is WHERE they are: 0.000..0.036 and
# 0.664..0.700 above the surface each baluster stands on. On the level runs that is a slab; on the
# rakes it is the same slab in the flight's own rotated frame, which is exactly how the plinths
# and the rails above them are placed. The band catches the foot of the plinth and the top of the
# cap with them, which is what a metal shoe wrapping a stone one looks like.
let stair_fix_a_sec = union {
    box 0.00 -0.002 -0.12   6.45 0.038 0.12
    box 0.00  0.662 -0.12   6.45 0.702 0.12
}
let stair_fix_b_sec = union {
    box 0.00 -0.002 -0.12   2.30 0.038 0.12
    box 0.00  0.662 -0.12   2.30 0.702 0.12
}
let stair_fix_zone = union {
    translate { rotate { stair_fix_a_sec } z=-0.08154932 } 8.10 5.04 4.41
    translate { rotate { stair_fix_b_sec } z=0.08154932 }  8.10 5.22 5.04
    box 6.25 5.038 2.94    6.53 5.078 6.60
    box 6.25 5.702 2.94    6.53 5.742 6.60
    box 7.87 5.038 4.55    8.15 5.078 4.90
    box 7.87 5.702 4.55    8.15 5.742 4.90
    box 10.02 6.298 4.45  15.10 6.338 4.73
    box 10.02 6.962 4.45  15.10 7.002 4.73
    box  9.97 6.298 4.59  10.25 6.338 5.04
    box  9.97 6.962 4.59  10.25 7.002 5.04
}
let stair_fixings = mirror { intersection { stair_balusters stair_fix_zone } } axis=x
paint bronze where=stair_fixings below=0.035

# The panel key is ONE SHAPE PER PIER and it is not a box: it is the pier's own block shrunk by
# 0.06 on every face, caught in the panel's height band. Every surface inside a sunk panel — its
# floor and its four returns — is 0.09 in from the pier's face and so is 0.03 INSIDE that shrunk
# block; a voxel on the pier's own face sits about 0.01 in, which is 0.05 OUTSIDE it, well past
# the rules' own 0.02. So the porphyry stops dead at the arris of the recess. 0.06 and not 0.0225:
# the first pass shrank by half the recess and painted every pier solid red from top to bottom,
# because a voxel centre is up to half a voxel inside the face it belongs to and the 0.02 test
# then reaches the rest of the way. The margin has to be bigger than the voxel, not than the
# tolerance.
#
# THE SIGN IS POSITIVE AND THAT IS THE ONE THAT SHRINKS. `Op::Offset` evaluates as
# `eval(child) + by`, so a POSITIVE `by` requires a point to be that much deeper inside before
# it counts — it shrinks — and a NEGATIVE one grows. Written `by=-0.06` this rule painted a zone
# 0.06 BIGGER than the pier and every pier in the passage came back solid porphyry from plinth to
# soffit. See clips/facility/requests/stair.md; BRIEF.md's grammar line has been corrected since.
let stair_inlay_low = intersection { offset { stair_pier_body } by=0.06
                                     box  6.15 2.19 2.85    6.90 4.41 3.48 }
let stair_inlay_up  = intersection { offset { stair_tpier_body } by=0.06
                                     box 11.46 2.19 4.35   12.21 5.46 5.10 }
let stair_inlays = mirror {
    union { stair_inlay_low  translate { stair_inlay_low } 0 0 3.36
            stair_inlay_up   translate { stair_inlay_up }  0 0 1.80 }
} axis=x
paint porphyry where=stair_inlays below=0.02
